# Learning the Tabla

I started learning the tabla when I was five years old. At that age, it was honestly just something that became part of my routine - lessons, practice, learning new compositions and slowly getting more comfortable with the instrument as well as having fun. I did not really think about what I was getting from it beyond learning how to play.

I learnt in the **Benares Gharana** tradition under my guruji **Pandit Sanju Sahai**, the torchbearer of the gharana. I am incredibly grateful for his teaching and guidance throughout my life. After seeing him almost every Saturday for years, he has become much more than just a teacher and genuinely felt like part of my family.

Looking back, one of the things I appreciate most about how I was taught is the emphasis on getting the basics right. On the tabla, the difference between a stroke sounding refined and sounding coarse can be surprisingly small: where the finger lands, how it leaves the skin, how the two drums are balanced and how cleanly the sound is allowed to resonate.

There was not much value in rushing ahead or playing fast just for the sake of playing something more difficult. A complicated composition only really sounds good if the individual strokes underneath it are clean.

The Benares style suits that emphasis particularly well. It is known for its open and resonant sound, strong use of both the bayan and the tabla as well as a particularly developed solo tradition. There is a lot of power in the style, but also a huge amount of control required to make that power sound deliberate rather than rough.

## Speaking What You Play

One of the things I find most interesting about tabla and Indian classical music in general is that you can actually **speak what you play**.

Indian classical melodic music has *Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni Sa*, roughly comparable to Do Re Mi in Western music. Tabla has its own spoken vocabulary through **bols**.

Different strokes are represented by syllables such as *Na*, *Ta*, *Tin*, *Dha* and *Dhin*. These can represent individual strokes or combinations of strokes played across the dayan and bayan. Because of that, an entire composition can be recited before it is played.

Speaking and playing were closely connected in the way I learnt. A composition would often be taught through its bols first, and being able to say it properly first helped with understanding the rhythm before trying to reproduce it on the drums.

When I was younger, I definitely found the actual playing more interesting than sitting there repeating bols. I appreciate that part of the process much more now. It is quite unusual to have such a direct relationship between language, rhythm and physical movement.

## Performing

For many years, I also performed at **Guru Purnima**.

Playing in front of an audience was nerve-racking when I was younger. Something I could play comfortably during a lesson suddenly became much harder when I was aware that a room full of people was listening.

Doing that year after year made the situation more familiar.

I would not say tabla was solely responsible for making me more confident, but regularly performing from a young age definitely helped. Later on, when I had to give presentations, speeches or speak in front of larger groups at school, the feeling of being watched was not completely new.

It also taught me that nerves and preparation are not necessarily opposites. You can know something properly and still feel nervous about performing it.

## Appreciating It Differently Now

Something I have appreciated more as I have got older is what learning music from such a young age actually involves.

I became particularly interested in this after reading more about learning and neuroplasticity. I noticed similar ideas when studying German: both music and languages involve repeatedly distinguishing sounds, recognising patterns, remembering structures and translating them into some form of response.

Research into music and second-language learning is obviously more complicated than saying either one simply “makes you smarter”, but there is evidence that both can affect how the brain adapts and processes information.

That made me look slightly differently at things I had been doing for years without really questioning them.

With tabla, I was learning to distinguish very small differences in sound, remember long rhythmic phrases, translate spoken bols into physical movements and keep track of a taal whilst listening to somebody else.

## Getting Back Into It

Admittedly, I have not practised nearly as much as I should have in recent years. There have been periods, particularly around GCSEs, A Levels and everything else going on, where I barely played at all.

Even when I was not practising much, though, I still kept an interest in tabla and in the music around it.

Recently I have been listening to much more Indian music again - particularly Indian classical music, ghazals, and poetry - and that has made me want to start playing more seriously again.

After not practising consistently, there are definitely things that feel less natural than they used to, and I know rebuilding that will take time.

So one of my goals now is simply to get back into practising more regularly and rebuild some of the consistency I had when I was younger.